What is a Deployment in Kubernetes?

A Deployment in Kubernetes is a resource used to manage and maintain a set of Pods (containers) in a desired state.

🔹 Simple Definition

👉 A Deployment ensures:

    Your application is running
      The correct number of instances (replicas) are available
      Updates happen smoothly without downtime


🔹 Real-Time Example

Imagine you deploy a web app (nginx):

You want 3 copies of your app running

If 1 pod crashes → Kubernetes automatically recreates it

If you update the app → it does rolling updates (zero downtime)




Flow 


Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods → Containers


Deploymes steps 


Recreate   pod down,  pod recrets zero down time 

Rolling Updates  ----   3 pods at time will not down,  2 pod down, 1 up older version,   2 new version, after 1 pods will updated new version    

Updates app without downtime  

Replaces old pods gradually



Rollback Support


If something breaks → revert to previous version



Scaling

kubectl scale deployment app --replicas=5


Self-Healing

If pod crashes → new pod is created automatically




Deployment                    vs Pod (Important Interview Point)



Feature                 Pod             Deployment 

Auto-healing            ❌                   ✅

Scaling                 ❌                   ✅

Rolling update          ❌                   ✅

Production use          ❌                   ✅





Rolling Updates manifest file 


apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-app-deployment
  labels:
    app: my-app
spec:
  replicas: 2
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate   # 👈 Deployment type
    rollingUpdate:
      maxUnavailable: 1
      maxSurge: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: my-app-container
        image: nginx:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-app-service
spec:
  type: NodePort   
  selector:
    app: my-app
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30080




recreate Manifestfile 


apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-app-deployment
  labels:
    app: my-app
spec:
  replicas: 2
  strategy:
    type: Recreate   
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: my-app-container
        image: nginx:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-app-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: my-app
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30080










Commands 


create deployment 


kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml



List Deployments

kubectl get deployments


kubectl get deploy -o wide


kubectl describe deployment my-app


Scale Deployment

kubectl scale deployment my-app --replicas=5


Update Deployment (Image Change)

kubectl set image deployment/my-app my-app=nginx:latest


Check Rollout Status

kubectl rollout status deployment/my-app


Rollback Deployment

kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-app


rollback to specific version 

kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-app --to-revision=2


Rollout History

kubectl rollout history deployment/my-app


Restart Deployment

kubectl rollout restart deployment/my-app



Pause / Resume Deployment

kubectl rollout pause deployment/my-app

kubectl rollout resume deployment/my-app



Delete Deployment

kubectl delete deployment my-app


Edit Deployment (Live)

kubectl edit deployment my-app


Get Pods from Deployment

kubectl get pods -l app=my-app



Logs for Deployment Pods

kubectl logs -l app=my-app


Exec into Pod

kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- /bin/bash
